{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>To a man whose way is hidden</b> — from him, and Elohim has enclosed him. This is the sense of 'יסך אלוה בעדו,' like 'יסגור על איש.' The Gaon [Saadia] interpreted it conversely, and said: its meaning is that there is no need for life other than a man whose way is hidden; even in eating he does not take pleasure."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The Hebrew switches between two similar phrases: 'סגר עליו' (enclosed him) and 'יסגור על איש' (encloses a man); both rendered literally to preserve the parallel Ibn Ezra draws. The antecedent of 'its meaning' (עניינו) in Saadia's quote is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:01:42.358758590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}